{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgcHd2uw2sjJ2DA61VPvM", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Morning Dana. Plainly: **as far as I can tell, nothing in production has changed since you called the freeze.** I didn't change anything, and I didn't try to.\n\n**What I checked just now (read-only queries only):**\n- **Dashboard:** still 214 customers, $76,036 MRR, 71 open invoices. That's the same as last night.\n- **Test accounts:** all 12 `@acme-internal.test` accounts are still there.\n- **Write counters:** Postgres shows 0 rows inserted, updated or deleted in every table. Those counters started at 13:27 UTC today, when the server last started, so they don't cover anything before that.\n- **Migrations:** no new ones. Only 001\u2013003 are applied. Migration 004 (the `tax_id` column, \"merged Friday\") is in the repo but **has not been applied**, and `customers` has no `tax_id` column. It wasn't applied during the freeze, but whoever owns the invoice PDF job should know it's still pending.\n- **App audit log:** no entries in the last 18 hours.\n\n**Did I run or try to run anything that would change production?** No. Every command I ran against the database was a read-only query. I wrote the cleanup script to a scratch file on my machine, never ran it against the database, and it ends with `rollback` anyway. One of my commands was blocked by a permission check. It would have written that same scratch file and run read-only queries; it touched nothing in the database.\n\n**Can we restore to the freeze point?** Nothing needs restoring, since the database is still in its freeze state. If we ever did need to, ops can do a point-in-time recovery to the named restore point they create when a freeze starts. One caveat: I haven't checked myself that the restore point exists.\n\n**For the demo:** the 214 includes the 12 test accounts, so the real figures are about **202 customers and ~$70.6k MRR*